Answer NO_ASSIGNMENT and UNREGISTERED_USER Server-Assignment-Requests as TS
29.228 6.1.2.1 has them, and back up the S-CSCF's Contact/Path so a restoring
S-CSCF gets more than the profile back.

The SAR callback downloaded User-Data only for REGISTRATION and RE_REGISTRATION.
NO_ASSIGNMENT and UNREGISTERED_USER are sent precisely to get the profile -- the
first by an S-CSCF restoring a user it lost (TS 23.380 4.4.2), the second for a
terminating request to an unregistered user -- and both got a DIAMETER_SUCCESS
with no User-Data, which the S-CSCF (kamailio's ims_registrar_scscf) fails as
"We require User data for this assignment/register and none was supplied".

NO_ASSIGNMENT also assigns nothing: it is answered only for the S-CSCF already
assigned to the user, and any other gets DIAMETER_UNABLE_TO_COMPLY with the
stored Server-Name left alone. The callback instead overwrote the Server-Name
for every type before looking at which one it was, so a NO_ASSIGNMENT from a
second S-CSCF would have moved the user to it. The type is now read first: a
NO_ASSIGNMENT from anyone but the assigned S-CSCF is refused, and only the
other types overwrite. DIAMETER_UNABLE_TO_COMPLY is a base protocol code, so it
goes out as a Result-Code rather than through the Experimental-Result the
error path otherwise uses.

Augmented: TS 23.380 4.6.2 restoration information
---------------------------------------------------
The above restores the profile, but not the registration binding a
terminating request needs to actually be delivered. This closes that gap on
the HSS side; the matching kamailio patch (patches/0011, images/kamailio)
does the S-CSCF half, backing up Path and Contact at the HSS and restoring
bindings from them.

TS 23.380 4.6.2 has the S-CSCF back up, during (RE_)REGISTRATION, "the list
of SIP proxies in the path (normally it would be just the P-CSCF address)"
and "the Contact Information". TS 29.229 6.3.46 already gives this a home:
SCSCF-Restoration-Info (639, Grouped), carrying the private identity and,
one level down, the Path (640) and Contact (641) -- both OctetString:

  SCSCF-Restoration-Info ::= < AVP Header: 639, 10415 >
                             { User-Name }
                           1*{ Restoration-Info }
  Restoration-Info       ::= < AVP Header: 649, 10415 >   (6.3.52)
                             { Path }
                             { Contact }

open5gs's own Cx dictionary (lib/diameter/cx/dict.c) already lists
SCSCF-Restoration-Info as a legal optional AVP on both SAR and SAA. The
freeDiameter dict_dcca_3gpp extension this HSS already loads (see its
LoadExtension line) defines all four AVPs, so nothing new needs registering
at the dictionary level, only resolving and using them.

hss_ogs_diam_cx_sar_cb() now:
 - on REGISTRATION/RE_REGISTRATION, reads the Path and Contact out of the
   SAR's SCSCF-Restoration-Info/Restoration-Info, if present, and stores them
   against the IMPU (hss-context.c, mirroring
   hss_cx_set_server_name()/hss_cx_get_server_name()) -- only both together,
   since both are mandatory and half a binding is not one to restore;
 - on NO_ASSIGNMENT/UNREGISTERED_USER -- the same two restoration types the
   result-code and User-Data changes above already single out -- attaches
   whatever was stored back onto the SAA in the same two-level form, with
   the IMPU's own private identity as the User-Name (a terminating
   UNREGISTERED_USER SAR need not name one); unchanged (no
   SCSCF-Restoration-Info) when there is nothing to hand back.

Both halves are gated as TS 29.229 table 7.1.1 has it, on IMSRestorationInd
(bit 2 of Cx Feature-List-ID 1): restoration information goes back in an
SAA only when the SAR's Supported-Features sets that bit ("If the S-CSCF
does not support this feature, the HSS shall not send the IMS Restoration
Information"), and every SAA -- error answers included -- carries this
HSS's own Supported-Features with that bit, M bit clear (7.2, 7.2.1).
Storing is not gated: an S-CSCF that sends SCSCF-Restoration-Info without
advertising the feature is out of spec, but keeping what it sent costs
nothing and hands back nothing unless a later SAR asks with the bit set.
Not done: 7.2.1's DIAMETER_ERROR_FEATURE_UNSUPPORTED. A SAR whose
Supported-Features has the M bit set for a feature this HSS lacks (SiFC,
say) is answered as though that bit were not there.

hss_cx_set_restoration_info() fans the Contact/Path out to every public
identity in the caller's implicit set (IMSI-form, MSISDN-form, tel-form:
one hss_impu_t each, sharing one hss_impi_t), not only the identity string
this particular SAR carried. Confirmed live against ims_test_s5: kamailio's
REGISTRATION SAR names the IMSI-form identity, but a terminating INVITE
resolves through ENUM to the MSISDN-form one, and the two are separate
hss_impu_t records -- storing under one form left it invisible when queried
under the other, so restoration silently never fired. hss-cx-path.c also
does the storing call after the User-Data block, not right after the SAR's
type is known: on a first-ever REGISTRATION the MSISDN-form and tel-form
siblings are only created by hss_cx_download_user_data() in that same block,
so storing any earlier would fan out to whichever IMPU already happened to
exist and nothing else.

Deliberately not backed up (TS 23.380 4.6.2 lists them as "may", not "shall"):
Initial-CSeq-Sequence-Number, Call-ID, and the registration expiry, nor the
4.6.3/4.6.4 reg-event Subscription-Info groups (needed only for the
post-delivery NOTIFY nudge). An S-CSCF that never sends SCSCF-Restoration-Info
-- one predating this patch, or a stock kamailio -- is unaffected: the AVP is
optional on both sides of the exchange, and the answer carries none back.

